  <!-- 1. First 10 minutes -->
  <section class="pb-section" id="first-10-minutes">
    <h2>Spilled on your MacBook? Do this in the first 10 minutes</h2>
    <p class="lead-in"><strong>Cut the power, drain it, and keep it off.</strong> These six steps give your Mac the best chance.</p>
    <ol class="big-steps">
      <li><span class="n">1</span><div><strong>Turn it off.</strong> Hold the power button (or Touch ID) for 10 seconds until the screen goes black. Don't stop to save your work.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">2</span><div><strong>Unplug everything.</strong> The charger first, then USB drives, hubs and screens.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">3</span><div><strong>Wipe the outside.</strong> Use a dry towel or paper towel. Don't press the keys. That pushes liquid deeper.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">4</span><div><strong>Tent it.</strong> Open the lid wide and stand it upside down like a tent, keyboard facing down, so liquid can run out.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">5</span><div><strong>Leave it off.</strong> Don't turn it on to test it. Don't charge it. Even off, the battery keeps part of the board powered, and that power speeds up corrosion.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">6</span><div><strong>Call us and bring it in.</strong> Ring <a href="tel:0245099248">02 4509 9248</a>. Tell us what spilled and when. The sooner we open it, the more we can save.</div></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Don't -->
  <section class="pb-section" id="dont">
    <h2>What not to do after a spill</h2>
    <p class="lead-in"><strong>Don't use rice, heat or air, and don't test it.</strong> Each one can turn a small fix into a big one.</p>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Don't</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Put it in rice</td><td>Rice can't reach inside, it doesn't stop corrosion, and its dust gets in. Apple says the same for wet iPhones.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Use a hair dryer or heater</td><td>Heat can damage parts and push liquid further in. Apple warns against external heat.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Blow compressed air into it</td><td>It drives liquid deeper under the keys and chips.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Turn it on "to check"</td><td>Power plus liquid can short chips that were still fine.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Charge it</td><td>Charging feeds power straight into the wet board.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wait for it to "dry out"</td><td>The outside dries in a day. Liquid trapped under chips does not, and the residue keeps corroding.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shake it</td><td>Shaking spreads liquid to parts it hadn't reached.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="source">Apple's advice on rice, heat and compressed air: <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-au/102643" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Apple Support</a>.</p>
  </section>

