  <!-- 4. What liquid does -->
  <section class="pb-section" id="what-happens">
    <h2>What water does inside a MacBook</h2>
    <p class="lead-in"><strong>Liquid harms a Mac in two ways: it shorts parts while there is power, and it corrodes the board for days or weeks after.</strong></p>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li><strong>Short circuits.</strong> Liquid joins tiny points on the board that should never touch. With power on, chips can burn out in seconds.</li>
          <li><strong>Corrosion.</strong> Minerals, sugar and salt stay behind when the water dries. With a little power and moisture, they eat the metal. You may see green or white crust.</li>
          <li><strong>It hides.</strong> Liquid gets trapped under chips and connectors, where you can't see it or dry it.</li>
          <li><strong>It spreads.</strong> On one real job, corrosion moved from the ports on one side of a MacBook Pro to the other side in three weeks.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <figure class="diagram">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 360 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="dgTitle dgDesc">
          <title id="dgTitle">Where liquid goes in a MacBook</title>
          <desc id="dgDesc">Simplified top view of a MacBook base. Liquid on the keyboard runs down onto the main board under the keyboard, and liquid near the ports reaches the port chips.</desc>
          <rect x="10" y="10" width="340" height="230" rx="18" fill="#F5F7FA" stroke="#94A3B8" stroke-width="2"/>
          <rect x="40" y="28" width="280" height="110" rx="8" fill="#fff" stroke="#CBD5E1" stroke-width="2"/>
          <g fill="#E2E8F0">
            <rect x="52" y="40" width="256" height="16" rx="3"/><rect x="52" y="62" width="256" height="16" rx="3"/><rect x="52" y="84" width="256" height="16" rx="3"/><rect x="52" y="106" width="256" height="20" rx="3"/>
          </g>
          <rect x="95" y="36" width="170" height="70" rx="6" fill="none" stroke="#1D4ED8" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="6 5"/>
          <text x="165" y="92" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#1D4ED8">Main board (under keys)</text>
          <rect x="110" y="150" width="140" height="70" rx="8" fill="#fff" stroke="#CBD5E1" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="180" y="190" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#475569">Trackpad, battery below</text>
          <rect x="10" y="60" width="14" height="22" rx="3" fill="#0F172A"/><rect x="10" y="90" width="14" height="22" rx="3" fill="#0F172A"/>
          <text x="30" y="128" font-size="10" fill="#0F172A">Ports</text>
          <path d="M248 14c0 0-8 10-8 15a8 8 0 0 0 16 0c0-5-8-15-8-15z" fill="#38BDF8"/>
          <path d="M248 40 L244 64" stroke="#0EA5E9" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
          <path d="M40 74 L26 74" stroke="#0EA5E9" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>
          <defs><marker id="arr" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="4" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 8 4 0 8z" fill="#0EA5E9"/></marker></defs>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Simplified layout. On MacBooks the main board sits under the keyboard, so a keyboard spill can reach it in seconds. Liquid near the side ports often kills the chips that run USB-C and charging.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <h3>Which liquids do the most damage</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Liquid</th><th>Why it hurts</th><th>Risk</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Plain water</td><td>Shorts live parts. Tap and rain water leave minerals behind.</td><td><span class="risk r1">Lower</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Black coffee or tea</td><td>Mildly acidic, leaves a stain and residue.</td><td><span class="risk r2">Medium</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Coffee with milk or sugar, juice, soft drink</td><td>Sticky sugar and fat hold moisture against the board.</td><td><span class="risk r3">High</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Beer or wine</td><td>Sugar and acid, and often a big spill.</td><td><span class="risk r3">High</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Salt water or pool water</td><td>Salt and chlorine carry current well and corrode fastest.</td><td><span class="risk r4">Very high</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

