  <!-- 7. How we repair -->
  <section class="pb-section" id="how-we-repair">
    <h2>How we repair a water-damaged MacBook</h2>
    <p class="lead-in"><strong>We stop the corrosion first, then find and replace only the parts that failed.</strong> That is why it often costs a lot less than a new board.</p>
    <ol class="big-steps">
      <li><span class="n">1</span><div><strong>Battery off.</strong> We open the Mac and disconnect the battery first. This cuts the power that feeds corrosion.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">2</span><div><strong>Look closely.</strong> We check the liquid indicators and map every spot of corrosion under a microscope.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">3</span><div><strong>Ultrasonic clean.</strong> The board goes into an ultrasonic bath with a board-safe solution. It reaches under the chips where a brush can't. Then it is fully dried.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">4</span><div><strong>Find the dead parts.</strong> Power readings, circuit diagrams and board maps show which chips have failed.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">5</span><div><strong>Replace only what failed.</strong> Common ones after a spill: USB-C controller chips, charging chips, backlight chips and audio chips.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">6</span><div><strong>Test everything.</strong> Charging, every port, keyboard, trackpad, screen, speakers, microphones, camera, Wi-Fi and the battery.</div></li>
      <li><span class="n">7</span><div><strong>You get a report.</strong> What we found, what we fixed, and anything that may still be at risk. Need it for insurance? We write a formal report for $149.</div></li>
    </ol>
    <div class="honest-box">
      <h3>What we can't promise</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Liquid damage is hard to predict. Two spills that look the same can end very differently.</li>
        <li>Hidden corrosion can cause a new fault later. We warranty the parts we replace, but there is no long-term guarantee on a liquid-damaged Mac. See our <a href="/terms/">terms</a>.</li>
        <li>If a repair costs more than your Mac is worth, we tell you, and we focus on your files.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. Decide -->
  <section class="pb-section" id="decide">
    <h2>Repair it, replace it, or just save the files?</h2>
    <p class="lead-in"><strong>It depends on what still works, whether you have a backup, and whether you have AppleCare+.</strong></p>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Our advice</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>It still works after a spill</td><td>Back up, then have it cleaned. This is the cheapest time to act.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Some keys, ports or sound have stopped</td><td>Clean and replace the failed parts. Usually worth it if the Mac is otherwise good.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>It won't turn on and you need the files</td><td>Board repair to reach the files. Files first, then decide on the Mac.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>It won't turn on, files are backed up, you have AppleCare+</td><td>Apple may be cheaper: A$429. Go to Apple.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Old Mac and the repair costs more than it's worth</td><td>We save your files and move them to your new Mac.</td></tr>
