  </section>

  <!-- 9. Files -->
  <section class="pb-section" id="files">
    <h2>Will you lose your files after a spill?</h2>
    <p class="lead-in"><strong>Not if the board is repaired. A new board means your files are gone on most newer Macs.</strong></p>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li><strong>Macs with a T2 chip (2018 to 2020) and all M1-and-newer Macs</strong> keep their storage soldered to the board, and it is always encrypted. The key lives in the chip on that board.</li>
      <li>So if a shop or Apple swaps the board, <strong>the old files can't be read</strong>, even if the storage chips were fine.</li>
      <li>That is why we repair the original board. Even when the Mac is not worth fixing, we fix it enough to start, then copy your files off.</li>
      <li><strong>Older Macs</strong> (MacBook Air to 2017, MacBook Pro to 2015) have a removable drive, so the files can often be read in another machine.</li>
      <li>Know your Mac login password. With FileVault on, we need it to unlock your files.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="source">Source: <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-au/guide/security/sec4c6dc1b6e/web" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Apple Platform Security: volume encryption on Apple silicon and T2 Macs</a>.</p>
  </section>

